<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-3.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-3.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="778" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110250" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-3.jpeg 1385w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-3-300x278.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-3-1024x949.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-3-768x711.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>While dropping off to sleep last night, I was thinking about how much work it might be to add a world map to the <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/wse" data-cached-time="2025-01-18T09:48:07" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/ewpNVFl4z.png" onmouseenter="hoverLink(event)">WordPress Statistics for Emacs</a> thing.  Not because it&#8217;s useful, but because Jetpack has one, and I have to have <a screenshot=true href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/2025/01/16/wordpress-statistics-for-emacs/">feature parity</a>!!!</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-1.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-1.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="428" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110242" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-1.jpeg 2300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-1-300x153.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-1-1024x522.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-1-768x392.jpeg 768w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-1-1536x783.jpeg 1536w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-1-2048x1044.jpeg 2048w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>So that&#8217;s what the Jetpack one looks like&#8230;  Yeah, yeah, Mercator, but it does look kinda nice?</p>
<p>But then it occurred to me: Surely somebody has made an SVG world map with nice markup?  And <a href="https://simplemaps.com/resources/svg-world" data-cached-time="2025-01-18T09:13:04" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/ewpUJaUk8.png" onmouseenter="hoverLink(event)">yes</a>!</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-4.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-4.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="67" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110251" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-4.jpeg 1372w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-4-300x24.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-4-1024x82.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-4-768x62.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>So I just have to read it in with <tt>libxml-parse-xml-region</tt>, adjust the colours to reflect view numbers with some <tt>dom-by-id</tt> and then let Emacs render it:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-5.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-5.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="431" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110252" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-5.jpeg 1155w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-5-300x154.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-5-1024x526.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-5-768x394.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>Easy peasy.</p>
<p>The one complication is that not all countries are contiguous, and you can&#8217;t have the same ID on several elements, so how do they solve that?</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-6.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-6.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="125" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-110253" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-6.jpeg 1376w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-6-300x45.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-6-1024x153.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/2025-01-18-6-768x115.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>*sigh*</p>
<p>So non-contiguous countries don&#8217;t have IDs at all, but have a class grouping them&#8230;  using the country name!  So you have to have not only country codes, but a mapping to country names, and hope you&#8217;re using the same mapping that they&#8217;re using (i.e., &#8220;United States&#8221; and not &#8220;United States of America&#8221;, etc (and can class names even have spaces?)).</p>
<p>I should demand a refund!  For that free map!  The nerve of some people!</p>
<p>Anyway.</p>
